      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>The 15-Year Commutation Recovery Dilemma: Union Petitions for 12 Years</h2>
        <p>
          One of the most intensely contested aspects of pension policy in India relates to the <strong>commutation of pension</strong>. Under current statutory provisions, a retiring government employee is permitted to commute up to a maximum of <strong>40%</strong> of their basic pension in exchange for an immediate, tax-exempt lump-sum disbursement.
        </p>
        <p>
          To recover this advance, the government deducts the commuted portion from the pensioner's monthly bank credit for a period of exactly <strong>15 years (180 months)</strong>. Following the completion of 15 years, the pension is legally restored to its full 100% nominal value.
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>Why Retiree Associations Demand a 12-Year Restoration Window:</h4>
          <p>
            When the 15-year recovery threshold was established decades ago, government lending interest rates were considerably higher, and average life expectancy tables placed different risks on actuarial capital. Independent financial calculations show that the government recovers both the principal commuted sum and the cumulative compound interest well within <strong>11 to 12 years</strong>. 
          </p>
          <p>
            Retaining deductions through year 15 results in excess recoveries from senior citizens aged 70 and older. The Joint Consultative Machinery (JCM) has strongly submitted that the 8th Pay Commission must officially lower the restoration threshold from 15 years to <strong>12 years</strong>, returning hundreds of crores directly to senior retirees.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Supplementary Age-Based Pensions for Super-Senior Citizens</h2>
        <p>
          Recognizing the exponential inflation in chronic geriatric healthcare, domestic nursing assistance, and prescription medication, the 6th and 7th Central Pay Commissions introduced a progressive tier of supplementary pension allowances once a retiree crosses the threshold of 80 years.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under the 8th CPC, these additional percentages will be calculated directly on the <em>newly revised, significantly higher basic pension</em>:
        </p>
        <ul class="editorial-list">
          <li><strong>Age 80 to less than 85 Years:</strong> Entitled to an additional <strong>20%</strong> of the revised basic pension.</li>
          <li><strong>Age 85 to less than 90 Years:</strong> Entitled to an additional <strong>30%</strong> of the revised basic pension.</li>
          <li><strong>Age 90 to less than 95 Years:</strong> Entitled to an additional <strong>40%</strong> of the revised basic pension.</li>
          <li><strong>Age 95 to less than 100 Years:</strong> Entitled to an additional <strong>50%</strong> of the revised basic pension.</li>
          <li><strong>Centenarians (100 Years and above):</strong> Entitled to a full <strong>100%</strong> top-up (effectively doubling their baseline pension).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Furthermore, multiple parliamentary committees and veteran organizations have formally urged the commission to institute an introductory <strong>5% or 10% supplementary slab starting at age 65 or 70</strong>, acknowledging that vulnerability and healthcare costs rise steeply long before an octogenarian birthday.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Fixed Medical Allowance (FMA) and CGHS Modernization</h2>
        <p>
          For central pensioners residing in mofussil towns, rural districts, or non-metro belts not served by dispensaries under the <strong>Central Government Health Scheme (CGHS)</strong>, the government remits a monthly <strong>Fixed Medical Allowance (FMA)</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under the 7th CPC, the FMA was maintained at a nominal rate of <strong>₹1,000 per month</strong>. In the context of modern outpatient consultations, routine blood diagnostics, and escalating therapy bills, this sum has proven grossly inadequate. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Representations forwarded to the Department of Expenditure urge that the 8th Pay Commission raise the base Fixed Medical Allowance to at least <strong>₹2,000 to ₹3,000 per month</strong>, indexed with future inflation, alongside broad cashless empanelment of district-level hospitals.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Unified Pension Scheme (UPS) vs Old Pension Scheme (OPS) Parity</h2>
        <p>
          The broader context of the 8th Pay Commission includes the introduction of the <strong>Unified Pension Scheme (UPS)</strong> for employees recruited post-2004 who were previously governed exclusively by the market-linked National Pension System (NPS). 
        </p>
        <p>
          Because the UPS guarantees an assured pension pegged at <strong>50% of the average basic pay drawn in the last 12 months</strong> for those with 25+ years of qualifying service—along with an assured family pension of 60%—it relies heavily on the pay matrix cells established by the 8th CPC. Any upward adjustment in the pay scales directly broadens the terminal corpus and safety net for the modern civil workforce, building institutional equity across generations.
        </p>
      </section>

